## 7. Skeleton assembly order

For IMPLEMENT, build the file in this order — each step is independently checkable:

1. `$schema`, `$id`, `title`, `description`, `type: "object"`
2. `$defs` primitives (§1) — then run `check_schema`
3. `$defs` objects: `provenance`, `confidence`, `retraction`, `quarantine`, `deprecation`
4. `$defs.legal_transition` — 19 `anyOf` branches generated from `design.md` §4.2's table
5. `$defs.promotion` incl. the `INV-FSM-2` binding clauses
6. Top-level `properties` (§2) + `required` (the 11 `R` rows)
7. The five conditional `allOf` clauses: promotion-presence + four lifecycle-detail clauses
8. `INV-EV-1` clause (`evidence_refs` `minItems` conditional on status)
9. `INV-TZ-1` clause
10. `"unevaluatedProperties": false` — **last**, and re-run the full corpus after adding it; this
    keyword is the one most likely to over-reject, and adding it last isolates the blame

